          "claim": "1.1 [Completeness: Fourier coefficients along the circles] Suppose $F\\in L^2(S^2,\\sigma)$ is orthogonal to every $Y_{lm}$, and for $k\\in\\mathbb Z$ and $\\theta\\in(0,\\pi)$ define $G_k(\\theta):=\\frac1{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}\\int_0^{2\\pi}F(\\Phi(\\theta,\\varphi))e^{-ik\\varphi}\\,d\\varphi$; by [F6] and Cauchy–Schwarz [F7] each $G_k$ is defined for almost every $\\theta$, and $\\int_0^\\pi|G_k(\\theta)|^2\\sin\\theta\\,d\\theta\\le\\int_0^\\pi\\int_0^{2\\pi}|F(\\Phi(\\theta,\\varphi))|^2\\,d\\varphi\\,\\sin\\theta\\,d\\theta<\\infty$, so $G_k\\in L^2(\\sin\\theta\\,d\\theta)$ and, after the reparametrisation $t=\\cos\\theta$, $G_k\\in L^2((-1,1),dt)$. For $l\\ge k\\ge0$, [F2] and [F5] compute $\\langle F,Y_{lk}\\rangle=b_{lk}\\int_0^\\pi G_k(\\theta)w_{lk}(\\cos\\theta)\\sin\\theta\\,d\\theta=b_{lk}\\int_{-1}^1G_k(t)w_{lk}(t)\\,dt$; hence $\\int_{-1}^1G_k(t)w_{lk}(t)\\,dt=0$ for every $l\\ge k$, and by [F3] $G_k=0$ almost everywhere for every $k\\ge0$. For $k<0$ write $m:=-k>0$; the convention gives $\\overline{Y_{l,-m}}=(-1)^mY_{lm}$, so orthogonality of $F$ to $Y_{l,-m}$ gives $0=(-1)^m\\int_{S^2}F\\,Y_{lm}\\,d\\sigma$, whose rectangle form is $b_{lm}\\int_0^\\pi G_{-m}(\\theta)w_{lm}(\\cos\\theta)\\sin\\theta\\,d\\theta$ with $0\\le m\\le l$; by [F3] again, $G_{-m}=G_k=0$ almost everywhere. So all circle Fourier coefficients of $F$ vanish for almost every $\\theta$. [given, F2, F3, F5, F6, F7, algebra]",
